What really happens when you run docker run?

When you use the Docker CLI to create a container, it’s not just spinning up some magic in the background — it follows a well-orchestrated pipeline of low-level components and system calls.

Here’s the behind-the-scenes breakdown:
🔹 The Docker CLI translates your command into a RESTful API request.
🔹 This request is sent to the Docker Daemon (dockerd).
🔹 The Docker Daemon:
  •Checks if the image exists locally.
  •If not, pulls it from the Docker Registry.
  •Then calls containerd to run the container.
🔹 containerd:
•Converts the image into an OCI-compliant bundle (includes rootfs + config).
•Passes it to containerd-shim, which handles I/O, logging, and lifecycle.
🔹 The shim invokes runC — the runtime that:
•Sets up namespaces (for isolation)
•Applies cgroups (for resource limits)
🔹 runC uses libcontainer to talk directly to the Linux kernel, which creates the actual isolated container process.
💡 This deep stack of tools ensures each container behaves like a separate, lightweight virtual system.
